# Dopant-Free Hole-Transporting Materials for Stable and Efficient Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Molecularly engineered novel dopant-free hole-transporting materials for perovskite solar cells (PSCs) combined with mixed-perovskite (FAPbI3 )0.85 (MAPbBr3 )0.15 (MA: CH3 NH3+ , FA: NH=CHNH3+ ) that exhibit an excellent power conversion efficiency of 18.9% under AM 1.5 conditions are investigated. The mobilities of FA-CN, and TPA-CN are determined to be 1.2 × 10-4 cm2 V-1 s-1 and 1.1 × 10-4 cm2 V-1 s-1 , respectively. Exceptional stability up to 500 h is measured with the PSC based on FA-CN. Additionally, it is found that the maximum power output collected after 1300 h remained 65% of its initial value. This opens up new avenue for efficient and stable PSCs exploring new materials as alternatives to Spiro-OMeTAD.

Molecularly engineered novel dopant-free hole-transporting materials for perovskite solar cells (PSCs) combined with mixed-perovskite $(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3})_{0.85}(\mathsf{M A P b B r}_{3})_{0.75}$ (MA: $C H_{3}N H_{3}^{+}$ , FA: $N H=C H N H_{3}^{+})$ that exhibit an excellent power conversion efficiency of $18.9\%$ under AM 1.5 conditions are investigated. The mobilities of FA-CN, and TPA-CN are determined to be $\mathsf{1}.2\times\mathsf{10^{-4}c m^{2}V^{-1}}\mathsf{s^{-1}}$ and $\mathsf{1.1}\times\mathsf{10^{-4}c m^{2}V^{-1}}\mathsf{s^{-1}}$ , respectively. Exceptional stability up to ${\mathfrak{s o o h}}$ is measured with the PSC based  on FA-CN. Additionally, it is found that the maximum power output collected after $\mathtt{l}300\mathtt{h}$ remained $65\%$ of its initial value. This opens up new avenue for efficient and stable PSCs exploring new materials as alternatives to Spiro-OMeTAD.
The organic–inorganic perovskites with the composition of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(A=CH_{3}N H_{3}}^{+}$ (MA), ${\mathrm{NH}}{=}\mathrm{CHNH}_{3}{}^{+}$ (FA), $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ ; $\mathrm{B}=\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ ; $\b{\mathrm{X}}=\b{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ , and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ) have become one of the most promising photovoltaic materials due to their remarkable optical and electronic properties.[1–4] The power conversion efficiencies (PCE) of perovskite solar cells have been quickly improved from $3.8\%$ in 2009 to the current world record of $22.1\%$ .[5–17] There are several perovskite solar-cell device architectures reported, but the most efficient, with PCE over $20\%$ , is based on the $\mathrm{{\n-i-p}}$ structure, where the perovskite acts as an intrinsic semiconductor, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as an electron acceptor (n-type layer), and poly(triarylamine) or $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,Ndi- $p$ -methoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobi­ fluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) as a hole transporter (p-type layer). To date, SpiroOMeTAD has been the most widely used hole-transport material (HTM) molecule; nevertheless, it suffers from low conductivity in its pristine form and therefore requires dopants/additives, such as cobalt complexes, 4-tert-butylpyridine (TBP), and lithium bis(trifluoromethyl sulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI).[7–9,12,15–20] Although significant development toward improved perovskite-solar-cell (PSC) efficiency has been realized, the stability of the Spiro-OMeTAD HTM is still limited due to dopants. To improve the stability of the PSCs, dopant-free HTMs are paramount.[21–41] Recently, Huang et al. reported a high PCE of $18.6\%$ by using a dopantfree HTM with a $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{3h}}$ symmetrical truxene core in a planar p–i–n-type perovskite solar cell.[40] They designed a dopant-free HTM with a planar, rigid, and fully conjugated molecule that exhibits excellent hole mobility and desired surface energy to the perovskite underlayer. Cheng et al. also reported a new ionic molecular material, dopant-free M7-TFSI, exhibiting a PCE of $17.4\%$ , which was derived from the deep highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level with high hole mobility and high conductivity. However it seems no group has been able to make highly stable devices using an organic HTM under 1 sunlight illumination $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ condition yet.
Here, we report molecularly engineered star-shaped D–π–A molecules incorporating a rigid quinolizino acridine (FA-CN) and a flexible triphenylamine (TPA-CN) as the central unit and donor part, akyl-substituted terthiophene as a conjugated bridge, and malononitrile as an electronacceptor part. Benzo[1,9]­quinolizino[3,4,5,6,7-defg]acridine (FA) is widely used in molecular semiconductors due to its planarity, which enhances $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, thereby facilitating charge transport.[42] On the other hand, the malenonitile moiety, one of the strongest electron acceptors, can stabilize the HOMO energy level.[43] PSCs using mixed-perovskite $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ (MA: $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ , FA: ${\mathrm{NH}}{=}\mathrm{CHNH}_{3}{}^{+}$ ), and the FA-CN and TPA-CN as dopant-free HTMs exhibited excellent PCEs of $18.9\%$ and $17.5\%$ under full sun illumination, respectively. The stability of the unsealed devices based on dopant-free FA-CN and TPA-CN is significantly enhanced compared to the doped Spiro-OMeTAD under light-soaking $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) conditions.
The quinolizino acridine/triphenylamine-based central units and the thiophene-based conjugated linkers were synthesized according to the literature and the synthetic routes are displayed in Scheme S1 (Supporting Information).[42–44] The prepared central donor unit was connected to the conjugated linkers by Pd-catalyzed Suzuki coupling reaction. The corresponding aldehyde derivative was obtained by Vilsmeier–Haack reaction using $\mathrm{POCl}_{3}$ /dimethylformamide. Finally, the Knoevenagel condensation of the aldehyde derivative with malononitrile in the presence of triethylamine led to the final molecules FA-CN and TPA-CN (Figure 1).
The UV–vis absorption and emission spectra of FA-CN and TPA-CN molecules in dichloromethane $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right)}$ solution are shown in Figure 2a. FA-CN displayed absorption bands at maxima of 418 and $527~\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the absorption bands of TPA-CN are blueshifted and less intense when the triphenylamine-based core is used instead of quinolizino acridine due to the less delocalized $\pi$ electrons. From the intersection of the absorption and emission spectra, the zero–zero transition energy $(E_{0-0})$ is estimated to be 1.99 and $2.03\mathrm{eV}$ for FA-CN and TPA-CN, respectively. The UV–vis spectra of the perovskite $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ films, with and without the new HTMs, were measured and compared with Spiro-OMeTAD data, as shown in Figure 2b. As shown in Figure 2b, in the presence of the new HTM, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ /perovskite/HTM films showed a significant enhancement in absorption between 400 and $560\mathrm{nm}$ , which is attributed to the colored FA-CN and TPA-CN molecules. In the case of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2I}$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD film, the slightly improved absorption is due to the oxidized Spiro-OMeTAD that has absorption in the visible region.[19] The redox properties of the two HTMs were determined by cyclic voltammetry (CV), and the measurements were done in dry $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ solution containing $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate $((n{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9})_{4}\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{PF}_{6}))$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The data are summarized in Table 1. The (HOMO) energy levels are estimated to be $-5.30$ and $-5.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ (vs vacuum) for FA-CN and TPA-CN, respectively, indicating that the hole injection from the perovskite to the HTM is feasible (Figure 2c). The TPA-CN molecule with the triphenylamine-based core is found to be lower in energy compared with FA-CN due to its weaker electron-donating effect.
In order to investigate the phase formation of new HTMs in the neat films, X-ray diffraction (XRD) for thin films was performed, as shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). To avoid the influence of the perovskite layer, all the films were deposited on silicon wafers. As evidenced from the XRD patterns and shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the FA-CN centered HTM thin film is in an amorphous phase and showed no diffraction peaks. The broad feature in the XRD profiles at around $24^{\circ}$ indicates a $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of $3.{\bar{7}}{\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ This distance is indicative of an ideal face-on organization with columnar stacks standing up the surface, whereas the same processed TPA-centered HTM thin film demonstrated more obvious crystalline phase with multiple diffraction peaks.
The optimized geometries for the new HTMs, along with some geometrical parameters, are presented in Figure S3a,b (Supporting Information). The main difference between the two HTM molecules is the core region. For FA-CN, the core is a co-planarized triphenylamine, which is a completely flat conjugated system, whereas the sterically hindered phenyl rings produce a typical propeller shape in TPA-CN. It can be expected that the difference between molecular structures will influence their packing during the formation of the solid films. To understand the nature of the frontier orbitals, the HOMO (highest occupied molecular orbital) and LUMO (lowest unoccupied molecular orbital) pictograms for the new HTMs are presented in Figure S3c,d (Supporting Information). In both molecules, the HOMO orbitals are mostly oriented in the central FA or TPA cores with vanishing influence from the peripheral thiophene rings. Due to the enhanced conjugation of the FA core, the antibonding combination of its HOMO orbital is more delocalized than that of TPA core. As for the LUMO orbitals, their delocalization complements that of the HOMO orbital (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The extension of these orbitals is mainly localized around the 2-methylenemalononitrile. As a result, it is reasonable to expect that the influence of the 2-methylenemalononitrile in the arms should be more significant for the LUMO energies, such that they are more influential on the spectroscopic properties.
The hole drift mobilities of pristine FA-CN, TPA-CN, and Spiro-OMeTAD were measured on organic field-effect transistor (OFET) substrates and can be found in Table 1 and Figure S4 (Supporting Information). The charge-carrier mobility was calculated from a linear fit (Ohm’s law). The mobility of FA-CN and TPA-CN were determined to be $1.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $1.1\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ respectively, which are higher than that of Spiro-OMeTAD $(\mu=2.3\mathrm{\dot{\times}}10^{-5}\mathrm{\dot{c}m^{2}V^{-1}\mathrm{s^{-1})}}$ . The output characteristics (OC) of FA-CN showed a good saturation at a drain– source voltage of $80\ \mathrm{V},$ and the transfer characteristics (TC) were recorded at exactly the same voltage, indicating excellent transistor characteristics. Although the OC of TPA-CN was not very good, the TC was similar with that of FA-CN. The engineered HOMO energy level and high hole mobility of these two molecules make them good candidates to work as dopant-free HTMs in PSCs.
For the device preparation, the deposition of $(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})_{0.85}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.15}$ on mesoporous- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ film was performed through single-step solution processing with antisolvent engineering method. As seen from the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image, the prepared perovskite film exhibited a dense and well-organized grain structure over the surface of the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electrode (Figure S5a, Supporting Information). After that, FA-CN/TPA-CN $(17\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ was deposited by spin coating from tetrachloroethane solution at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The perovskite layer was uniformly covered by a thin layer of an HTM (Figure 3a,b and Figure S6b, Supporting Information). The two images in Figure 3 show the coverage of the $^{\mathrm{a})}E_{0-0}$ was estimated by the intersection of the absorption and emission spectra in ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ solution; b)Redox potential of the molecules were measured in ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ solution with $0.1~\mathrm{~\textmu~}$ $(n{\cdot}C_{4}\mathsf{H}_{9})_{4}\mathsf{N}(\mathsf{P F}_{6})$ with a scan rate of $50\ m\vee\ s^{-1}$ (vs Fc/Fc+); $^{\mathrm{c})}E_{\mathsf{H O M O}}$ was calculated from $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{\Phi_{{ox}}}}^{0}$ (V) versus $\mathsf{F c}/\mathsf{F c}^{+}+0.62$ versus NHE $\mathrm{~+~}4.44$ versus vacuum; $^{\mathrm{d})}E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}$ was calculated by $E_{\mathsf{H O M O}}$ and $E_{0-0}$ .
perovskite layer with the HTM. Figure 3b was acquired from the same regions as Figure 3a but with a lower beam energy. Since we used a higher beam energy of $3~\mathrm{kV}$ (Figure 3a), the electrons pass through the HTM layer and therefore most of the signal comes from the perovskite layer. By lowering the beam energy to $1\mathrm{keV},$ penetration depth of the electron significantly decreases, allowing the HTM layer to be visualized. Also, we found that the small crystals/highly ordered domains were formed on the top of the HTM layer (Figure 3a and Figure S6b, Supporting Information). According to the scanning transmission electron microscopy–energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometry (STEM-EDX) elemental maps (Figure S7, Supporting Information), these small crystals/highly ordered domains come from the perovskite layer. The cross-sectional SEM image further shows that the thickness of perovskite capping layer and the HTM layer (FA-CN and TPA-CN) are about 450 and $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 3c and Figure S5c, Supporting Information). It has to be noted that the Spiro-OMeTAD-based device needs a sufficient thickness of over $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for the HTM layer. Finally, the devices were completed by the evaporation of a thin gold layer as the back contact.
Figure 3d compares the photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the perovskite films with and without HTM. It is noted that all the bilayers with the new HTMs show a dramatic quenching with respect to the pristine perovskite with similar intensity. The PL quenching is reasonably attributed to the efficient hole transfer that happens at the perovskite/HTM interface. By fitting the broad PL spectra, two components are identified, one peaking at around $765{-}768~\mathrm{nm}$ and a redshifted one at around $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Since, at a $532~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation wavelength, the FA-CN/ TPA-CN themselves absorb $\approx10\%$ of the incident light, this redshifted peak is attributed to the intrinsic emission of the
FA-CN/TPA-CN that extends down to $900\ \mathrm{nm}$ (see Figure 2a). To better visualize this redshifted emission peak, please refer to the PL spectra in logarithmic scale in Figure S8a (Supporting Information). The absence of this peak when measuring the pristine perovskite excludes that this can be assigned to any perovskite midgap states emission. On the other side, the “blue” peak matches with the PL peak from the perovskite. This peak is quenched by $75\%$ and $77\%$ for FA-CN and TPA-CN, respectively. This reduction of the perovskite PL can be attributed to efficient hole transfer to TPA-CN and FA-CN. However, since both TPA-CN and FA-CN emission partially overlap with the perovskite one, and the same does their absorption profile, this numbers do not pretend to be quantitative, but still indicate that a significant quenching occurs. We also note, that considering the absorption profile in Figure 2b, the TPA-CN absorbs slightly higher than the FA-CN. This can be responsible for the relatively higher PL intensity of the peak at $800\mathrm{nm}$ for the TPA-CN.
The charge dynamics of the perovskite layer with and without HTM atop are investigated by time-resolved PL measurements, as shown in Figure 3e. The signal has been collected at $770\mathrm{nm}$ where the perovskite PL peaks. The pristine perovskite shows a long-living signal for which the decay is beyond the reach of our experimental time window, a result that is in agreement with previous reports.[45,46] The emission of the perovskite/HTM is visibly quenched. In particular, a fast component of around 2 ns has been revealed. This component can be due to hole transfer happening at the perovskite/HTM interface, slightly enhanced in amplitude for the FA-CN/perovskite interface. The results suggest that an efficient hole-transfer path is established at the perovskite/TPA-CN and FA-CN interfaces. It is worth noticing however, that a partial contribution of the FA-CN and TPA-CN emission cannot be ruled out, since they can also be partially directly excited. However, we note that their emission in Figure S8b (Supporting Information) reveals a completely different behavior, with a much faster intrinsic lifetime that cannot be resolved by our instrument ( $^{\cdot}{<}1$ ns), different from what is observed in Figure 3e.
Figure 4a shows the current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics of the solar cells based on FA-CN and TPA-CN, and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2. The reference cells using doped Spiro-OMeTAD were prepared for comparison. Under our standard conditions, the reference device fabricated with pristine Spiro-OMeTAD showed a PCE of only $7.5\%$ . However, the performance of the device can be dramatically improved to $19.2\%$ after adding ${\bf C}{\bf o}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complex, TBP, and LiTFSI into the Spiro-OMeTAD solution. By contrast, the device with dopant-free FA-CN displayed a PCE of $18.9\%$ under standard AM 1.5 illumination $(100\mathrm{~mw}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ), which is among the highest reported performances based on dopantfree HTMs so far, with a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $21.71\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $1.13\ \mathrm{V},$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.77. Under the same conditions, the device with dopant-free TPA-CN exhibited slightly lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ and finally displayed a relatively lower PCE of $17.5\%$ , which might be due to the less-efficient charge extraction and/or collection at the perovskite/HTM interface. It should be noted that the photovoltaic performance of the devices based on the dopant-free FA-CN and TPA-CN is competitive with that based on the doped Spiro-OMeTAD. The change of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ based on different HTMs was further confirmed by the incident photonto-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectrum shown in Figure 4b. The short-circuit current density integrated from the IPCE spectra were 21.2, 20.7, and $22.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for FA-CN, TPA-CN, and Spiro-OMeTAD, respectively, which is in agreement with those obtained for $J{-}V$ curves. The doped SpiroOMeTAD-based device gives higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ while the new HTMs show improved fill factor due to higher hole mobility and ultrathin films. A small hysteresis was observed in the $J{-}V$ curves between reverse and forward scans in all the cases. The lower performance in the forward scan is mainly due to the low FF. Figure S10 and Table S1–S3 (Supporting Information) provide statistical data on the reproducibility of the solar-cell performance. The average PCEs for perovskite solar cells based on FA-CN, TPA-CN, and Spiro-OMeTAD obtained from 40 devices were $17.2\pm0.98\%$ , $16.4\pm0.88\%$ , and $18.06\pm0.65\%$ , respectively. The small standard deviation points toward good device reproducibility. As seen from Figure 4c, $80\%$ of the TPA-CNbased devices showed PCE higher than $16\%$ , and half of them were more than $17\%$ . About $65\%$ of the FA-CN-based devices exhibited PCE above $17\%$ , and one third of them showed PCE higher than $18\%$ .
The stability of the PSCs is an important factor to determine their further commercialization. In Figure 5, we describe the maximum power output (MPO) decay of the devices with dopant-free FA-CN, TPA-CN, and doped Spiro-OMeTAD as HTMs prepared under the same conditions. During the measurements, all the unsealed devices were kept in argon ambience under a constant illumination of $100~\mathrm{\mw~cm^{-2}}$ . The devices were maintained at the maximum power point during aging, and the current–voltage curves were recorded automatically every $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ . The devices based on both dopant-free FA-CN and TPA-CN exhibited better stability than the doped Spiro-OMeTAD in particular with the case of FA-CN.[47,48] The performance of all the devices dramatically decreased at the beginning, while the efficiency of the doped Spiro-OMeTADbased device decreased rapidly during $100\mathrm{{h}}$ . Similar dynamics have been recently observed by Guarnera and co-workers, who demon­strated that a rapid degradation mechanism is activated by gold metal migration through the HTM to the perovskite layer.[49] Promising stability was observed for the FA-CN-based device, which was stable for $500\mathrm{~h~}$ after an initial drop in the first day, whereas the performance of the TPA-CN- and SpiroOMeTAD-based devices decreased continuously. After $1300\mathrm{~h~}$ of continuous light soaking, the MPO values based on FA-CN, TPA-CN, and Spiro-OMeTAD devices remained at $65\%$ , $25\%$ , and $15\%$ of their initial value, respectively. The FA-CN-based device showed superior photochemical stability under lightsoaking $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ conditions for $1300\mathrm{h}$ . We have demon­ strated that such a clear improvement in stability based on the new HTMs is due to their photochemical stability and the absence of additives.
In summary, we synthesized star-shaped D– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\pi$ –A-type hole-transporting materials coded as FA-CN and TPA-CN, incorporating a rigid quinolizino acridine and a flexible triphenylamine donors with akyl-substituted terthiophene, and malononitrile as electron acceptor. Novel HTMs exhibit suitable HOMO levels and high hole mobility in the dopant-free condition. Also, the optimized devices with dopant-free FA-CN and TPA-CN showed an impressive PCE of $18.9\%$ and $17.5\%$ under standard global AM 1.5 illuminations, which are comparable to that of the devices using doped Spiro-OMeTAD. Remarkably, the $18.9\%$ efficiency using FA-CN is among the highest PCEs reported for dopant-free HTMs in perovskite solar cells. In addition, we demonstrated significantly improved long-term stability of FA-CN devices tracking maxi­mum power output under the condition of full illumination. These results demonstrate that the stability of HTMs has a strong impact on the long-term stability of the overall device and we expect that this study will accelerate the exploration of new dopant-free HTMs.
## Experimental Section
Materials: The synthetic details of the new HTMs are shown in the Supporting Information.
Solar-Cell Fabrication: Etched FTO $(\mathsf{S n O}_{2};\mathsf{F})$ glass (Nippon Sheet Glass) was cleaned sequentially by sonication in a $2\%$ Hellmanex solution, acetone, and ethanol, followed by a $15\mathrm{\min}$ UV–ozone treatment. A $30\ n m$ thick $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ compact layer was deposited by spray pyrolysis at $450~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from a precursor solution of diluted titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) (TAA) solution (Sigma-Aldrich) in isopropyl alcohol. On the top of it, a $150\ n m$ mesoporous $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer was made by the spin-coating method using a commercially available $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ paste (Dyesol 30NRD). The substrates were sintered at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 30 min in air. Then, Li-doping of the mesoporous $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was done by spin coating a $0.03\mathrm{~m~}$ solution of LiTFSI (in acetonitrile) at 3000 rpm for $\rceil0\ s$ , followed by sintering at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20{\mathsf{m i n}}$ before use. The mixed perovskite precursor solution was prepared by dissolving $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(7.75~\mathsf{M})$ , FAI $(7.70~\mathsf{M})$ , $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ $(0.2~\mathsf{M})$ , and MABr $(0.2~\mathsf{M})$ in a anhydrous solvent DMF:DMSO $=4{:}7$ (volume ratio). The perovskite precursor solution was spin coated at 2000 rpm for ${\mathsf{10~s}},$ , followed by 6000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Trifluorotoluene $(\mathsf{l}\mathsf{l}0\upmu\mathsf{L})$ was dropped on the spinning substrate at $20\textsf{s}$ in the second step. The films were annealed at ${100~^{\circ}C}$ for 90 min in a glove box. The hole-transporting material, consisting of $17\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ FA-CN or TPA-CN in tetrachloroethane at $70~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , was spin coated on the top of the perovskite layer with a spin speed of 3000 rpm. For comparison, the devices using Spiro-OMeTAD as an HTM, consisting of $60\times10^{-3}$ m Spiro-OMeTAD, $30\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{m}$ LiTFSI, $198\times10^{-3}$ m TBP, and $1.8\times10^{-3}$ m of tris(2-(1H-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine) cobalt(III) tris(bis(trifluoromethyl sulfonyl)imide) (FK209) in chlorobenzene, were prepared by spin coating the corresponding solution with a spin speed of 4000 rpm. Finally, $80~\mathsf{n m}$ of Au was deposited by thermal evaporation as the back electrode.
PL Measurements: Time-resolved PL experiments were performed using a spectrophotometer (Gilden Photonics). For steady-state measurements, excitation with a lamp was used at $530~\mathsf{n m}$ and the signal was collected by a photomultiplier tube. For the dynamics, a pulsed source at $460\ \mathsf{n m}$ was used ${\mathsf P}\mathsf{s}$ diode lasers BDS-SM, pulse with $<100$ ps, from Photonic Solutions, $\approx]\mathsf{m}\mathbb{W}$ power, $20~\mathsf{M H z}$ repetition rate, $\approx500$ um spot radius) and the signal was recorded at $770~\mathsf{n m}$ by the time-correlated single-photon-counting detection technique with a time resolution of 1 ns. The samples were encapsulated to prevent any influence of oxygen or moisture on the measurement and sample degradation.
Photovoltaic Characterization: The current–voltage curves were measured under an $\mathsf{A M}1.5$ simulated light source connected to a source meter (Keithley 2400). The light intensity was calibrated with an NRELcertified KG5 filtered Si reference diode. $J{-}V$ curves were obtained at a scan rate of $25~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . The devices were measured by using a black mask with an active area of $0.76~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . The IPCE spectra were measured by using a commercially available instrument (ORIEL, IQE 200B). For stability tests, the unsealed solar cells (under Ar atmosphere) were subjected to constant light soaking at $100m\times100m^{-2}$ . During the tests, the devices were maintained at the maximum power output using electronic control at a temperature of about $45^{\circ}C$ . The light source was based on arrays of white-light-emitting diodes (LXM3-PW51 4000K, Philips). An automatic current–voltage measurement was made every $2\ h$ .
